    "description": "Vacancy Announcement MSD is an indigenous, non-profit civil society organization established in 1999 with a mission to improve the health, well-being, and socio-economic conditions of communities across Ethiopia. Over the past decades, MSD has evolved into a well-established development and humanitarian organization, delivering evidence-based, community-centered programs that respond to the evolving needs of vulnerable and underserved populations. MSD implements integrated programs across a broad range of thematic areas, including: Health and Nutrition: TB, HIV/AIDS, sexual and reproductive health (SRH), maternal, newborn and child health (MNCH), and nutrition. Education and Skills Development: Access to quality education, technical and vocational education and training (TVET), and skills development. Social Mobilization and Livelihoods: Economic strengthening, community mobilization, and prevention of harmful traditional practices (HTPs), gender-based violence (GBV), and female genital mutilation (FGM), Migration and Trafficking: Safe migration, protection, rehabilitation, and reintegration support for migrants, returnees, and victims of trafficking; Child Care and Development: Child protection, early childhood care and development, and infant and young child feeding. , Human Rights and Good Governance: Democratization, peace building, social accountability, civic engagement, and dialogue; Humanitarian and Emergency Response: Integrated assistance and protection services for communities affected by conflict, displacement, disasters, and other emergencies; and Environment and Climate Change: Environmental sustainability, climate resilience, and biodiversity conservation. Through these integrated programs, MSD works in close collaboration with federal and regional government institutions, communities, civil society organizations, development and humanitarian partners, and other stakeholders to strengthen local systems, expand access to essential services, promote inclusion and equity, and build resilient communities across Ethiopia. Project Background: The Border Security Programme (BSP) is a UK Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office (FCDO)-funded regional initiative designed to address organized immigration crime (OIC), strengthen border security, and contribute to safer borders and communities. The programme seeks to reduce the activities and influence of criminal networks involved in facilitating irregular migration, human smuggling, human trafficking, document fraud, and other forms of organized immigration-related crime. It adopts a comprehensive, multi-sectoral approach that combines institutional capacity strengthening, evidence-based decision-making, community participation, and protection support for individuals affected by organized immigration crime. MSD is looking for qualified and competent applicants for the following position: Position: GBV Case Management Officer – Border Security Programme Location: Metema Salary: Negotiable subject to organizational salary scale and project budget No. Required: 1 Gender: Both male and female are invited, and women are highly encouraged to apply. Term of Employment: Contract Job Summary The GBV Case Management Officer is responsible for providing comprehensive, survivor-centred, and confidential case management services to survivors of gender-based violence (GBV), particularly vulnerable women, girls, and migrant populations, under the Border Security Programme (BSP) in Metema and surrounding border communities. Based in Metema and reporting to the Migration/Protection Coordinator, the Officer ensures timely identification, assessment, and response to GBV-related protection concerns through individualized case management, psychosocial first-line support, safety planning, and facilitation of safe referrals to appropriate medical, psychosocial, legal, shelter, and other specialized services. The position works closely with relevant government institutions, health facilities, protection actors, shelters, community structures, and other service providers to strengthen GBV referral pathways and ensure coordinated and appropriate support for survivors. The Officer also contributes to identifying GBV risks and service gaps, conducting protection awareness activities, monitoring case follow-up, and maintaining accurate and confidential case documentation in accordance with established GBV case management standards. All interventions must uphold survivor-centred principles, confidentiality, informed consent, respect for survivors' choices, safeguarding, PSEAH, and do-no-harm approaches, ensuring that survivors' safety, dignity, and rights remain paramount. Key Duties and Responsibilities:- GBV Identification and Case Assessment: Identify and support GBV survivors through safe, confidential, and ethical identification, intake, assessment, and documentation procedures. Individual Case Management: Develop and implement individualized case action plans based on survivors' needs, priorities, choices, and informed consent, ensuring regular follow-up and case closure in accordance with GBV case management standards. Psychosocial Support: Provide psychological first aid (PFA), emotional support, and appropriate psychosocial interventions to survivors, ensuring a safe, respectful, and non-judgmental environment. Safety Planning and Risk Assessment: Conduct individualized safety and risk assessments and develop appropriate safety plans to reduce exposure to further violence, exploitation, intimidation, or retaliation. Referral and Service Coordination: Facilitate timely referrals to appropriate medical, legal, psychosocial, shelter, security, and other specialized services, ensuring continuity of care and follow-up. Survivor-Centered Support: Apply survivor-centered principles, including safety, confidentiality, respect, non-discrimination, informed consent, and the right of survivors to make their own decisions. GBV Prevention and Awareness: Support community awareness and sensitization activities on GBV prevention, women's and girls' rights, available services, and reporting and referral mechanisms. Coordination with Service Providers: Work closely with health facilities, social affairs offices, police, justice institutions, shelters, and specialized service providers to strengthen access to comprehensive GBV response services. Case Documentation and Reporting: Maintain accurate, confidential, and secure case management records and prepare regular activity and service delivery reports while protecting survivors' identities and personal information. Safeguarding and Capacity Building: Ensure compliance with GBV case management protocols, child safeguarding, PSEA, data protection, and Do No Harm principles, and provide technical guidance to relevant frontline workers on safe identification and referral.",
